{"from": "claim:lk-18-18-23-rich-ruler", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-19-16-rich-young-man", "note": "Same event; Luke alone calls the man 'a certain ruler' (archon); Matt 19:16 has 'a man', Mark 10:17 an unnamed man running and kneeling."}
{"from": "claim:lk-18-32-33-delivered-to-gentiles", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-20-19-mocked-scourged-crucified", "note": "Same passion prediction; Luke adds delivery to the Gentiles, shameful treatment and spitting; Matt 20:19 has mock, scourge, crucify, third day (spitting appears in Matt 26:67)."}
{"from": "claim:lk-18-35-43-blind-beggar-jericho", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mk-10-46-47-bartimaeus", "note": "Same blind beggar at Jericho; Mark names him Bartimaeus son of Timaeus, Luke leaves him unnamed."}
{"from": "claim:lk-18-35-43-blind-beggar-jericho", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-20-30-31-cry-son-of-david", "note": "Matt has two blind men as Jesus went out of Jericho; Luke has one unnamed man as he drew near Jericho (the entering/leaving difference; cf. the two Jerichos, Herodian new city and old tell)."}
{"from": "claim:lk-18-12-tithes-all", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-23-23-tithing-herbs", "note": "Same tithing custom; Luke 18:12's 'all that I get' states it more broadly than the herbs of Matt 23:23/Luke 11:42."}
{"from": "claim:lk-18-12-tithes-all", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-11-42-tithe-herbs", "note": "Same tithing custom within Luke."}
{"from": "claim:lk-18-12-fast-twice-week", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-06-16-fasting", "note": "Matt 6:16 records the hypocrites' fasts (disfigured faces); Luke 18:12 quantifies the Pharisee's fast at twice a week."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-4-214-216", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-18-02-05-judge-widow-pleading", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus's restatement of the Mosaic constitution has 'seven men to judge in every city', deciding cases locally - the institution the parable assumes (a city judge hearing a widow's case). A legal ideal from the Mosaic law, not a report of 1st-century practice; and it attests the institution, not that the parable was told."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-taanit-2-2", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-18-12-fast-twice-week", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Taanit 2:2 sets communal fasts on Monday, Thursday and the following Monday - fixed weekday fasts, so fasting twice in a week is a recognized pattern. Redacted c. 200 CE and later; it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly. Attests the custom, not that the parable was told. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "text:didache-8-1", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-18-12-fast-twice-week", "note": "Reception: Didache 8:1 knows 'the hypocrites'' fast on the second and fifth day of the week (Monday and Thursday) - twice weekly - and directs Christians to Wednesday and Friday instead; it says 'as the Lord commanded in His Gospel', so it depends on Gospel tradition and is linked as related_to, not attests."}
